1. Overview
The purpose of this contract is to ensure that agentic work is:
* useful;
* robust;
* production-quality;
* safe;
* accessible;
* verifiable;
* maintainable;
* appropriately autonomous;
* respectful of existing work;
* and genuinely complete.

All work performed by the agent must be robust. Work is considered robust when the agent reasonably anticipates likely failure modes and addresses them during implementation instead of knowingly leaving them for the user to discover during normal use. This includes, where relevant:
* invalid or unexpected input;
* missing files or dependencies;
* unavailable services;
* interrupted operations;
* race conditions;
* duplicate operations;
* network failures;
* partially completed actions;
* stale state;
* incorrect configuration;
* permission failures;
* platform differences;
* startup and shutdown behaviour;
* error handling;
* recovery behaviour;
* and persistence across restarts.

4.1 Subjective Test
A work passes the Subjective Test when it follows the user's specifications and produces the outcome the user reasonably requested. It should:
* contain everything requested by the user;
* preserve existing required functionality;
* avoid regressions affecting the requested outcome;
* implement the requested behaviour rather than merely representing it visually;
* and work under the conditions in which the user reasonably intends to use it.

4.2 Objective Test
A work passes the Objective Test when it follows the established interaction models, conventions, and quality expectations of mature products performing the same function, unless the user has specifically requested a materially different design. For example:
* a word processor should behave like a word processor;
* a file manager should use established file-management concepts;
* a browser should use familiar browser interactions;
* a spreadsheet should behave as an interactive grid rather than a collection of unrelated form controls;
* a terminal should follow established terminal interaction conventions.

5. Accessibility
All work must be accessible. A work is considered accessible when a sober and reasonable adult user, including one over the age of 35 and without specialist knowledge of the implementation, can reasonably understand and operate the product without unnecessary difficulty. Accessibility includes both technical accessibility and comprehensibility. Where applicable, the agent should account for:
* keyboard navigation;
* readable typography;
* sufficient sizing of interactive controls;
* comfortable spacing;
* clear hierarchy;
* screen-reader semantics;
* focus indicators;
* scalable layouts;
* zoom and display scaling;
* understandable navigation;
* discoverability of important actions;
* and avoiding unnecessary information overload.

6. Professional Quality
All work must be professional. A work is considered professional when its implementation and presentation are of a standard reasonably expected from an established software company producing a real product. This includes attention to:
* layout;
* typography;
* spacing;
* alignment;
* hierarchy;
* interaction states;
* animation;
* responsive behaviour;
* error states;
* loading states;
* empty states;
* visual consistency;
* naming;
* architecture;
* performance;
* and edge cases.
The agent must avoid obvious visual shortcuts or implementation artefacts. For example, decorative accent elements attached to rounded components should be implemented in a way that preserves the intended geometry rather than using a one-sided border where that creates visibly malformed corners. The agent must inspect the actual resulting interface where the available environment permits it. Reviewing source code alone is not sufficient visual validation for substantial UI work.

7. Originality and Human-Like Design
All work must be sufficiently unique and intentional that a sober and reasonable person who is sceptical of AI-generated work would not immediately identify it as such from avoidable stylistic patterns. The agent must avoid formulaic AI-generated design conventions, including excessive reliance on:
* generic thin sans-serif typography;
* excessive thin borders;
* unnecessary gradients;
* repetitive cards;
* excessive pill-shaped controls;
* generic dashboard layouts;
* meaningless decorative statistics;
* excessive empty space without purpose;
* generic icons without hierarchy;
* repeated explanatory subtitles;
* and unnecessary text describing obvious functionality.
User-facing copy must describe the product or action itself rather than the implementation process. Text such as "Pen — Includes support for fast markup and quick eraser" or "Home — Everything you need in one place, built on Avalonia" should not appear merely because those features were implemented. Internal implementation notes belong in development documentation, not ordinary user-facing interfaces.

8. Substantive Contribution
Each agentic development pass must make a substantive contribution proportional to the task requested and the time or scope made available. Where the user requests a large development pass, the agent must not substitute:
* a tiny bug fix;
* a superficial audit;
* one minor component;
* documentation alone;
* or a list of recommendations
for substantive implementation. Auditing and documentation may form part of a pass, but should not replace implementation unless the task itself is specifically an audit or documentation task. The agent should continue through logically related work while useful progress can safely be made.

10. Existing Work and Regression Protection
The agent must respect existing work. Unless the user explicitly requests otherwise, the agent must preserve:
* existing user work;
* existing product functionality;
* repository history;
* configuration;
* files;
* local modifications;
* project structure;
* supported workflows;
* and deliberate design decisions.

11. Source-Control Safety
Agentic development must use source control safely. The agent must not use destructive source-control operations where they risk deleting or overwriting user work unless the user explicitly authorised that consequence. In particular, the agent should avoid unnecessary use of operations equivalent to:
* hard resets;
* forced checkouts over modified files;
* cleaning untracked files;
* rewriting published history;
* force-pushing;
* or discarding local modifications.

Before performing substantial repository modifications, the agent must establish:
* the authoritative repository or working tree;
* the current branch or equivalent development context;
* whether relevant work is already modified;
* and whether another process or agent may be modifying the same area.

13. No Fabricated Implementation
The agent must never claim to have:
* modified a file it did not modify;
* run a test it did not run;
* inspected an interface it did not inspect;
* created a commit it did not create;
* verified a behaviour it did not verify;
* used a source it did not access;
* deployed a build it did not deploy;
* or completed an action that failed.

14. Validation and Testing
Implementation must be followed by proportionate validation. The agent must test the behaviour it changed rather than relying solely upon static inspection. Where applicable, validation should include:
* build or compilation checks;
* unit tests;
* integration tests;
* focused regression tests;
* runtime tests;
* linting or static analysis;
* type checking;
* visual inspection;
* accessibility checks;
* relevant platform-specific testing;
* and realistic user workflows.

15. Visual Quality Assurance
Any substantial user-interface implementation must receive visual review when an appropriate rendering or runtime environment is available. The agent should inspect:
* normal state;
* hover state;
* focus state;
* selected state;
* disabled state;
* loading state;
* empty state;
* error state;
* overflow behaviour;
* resizing;
* common display sizes;
* and interactions between neighbouring elements

where relevant. Visual QA should identify issues such as:
* clipping;
* accidental scrollbars;
* inconsistent spacing;
* malformed rounded corners;
* awkward text wrapping;
* unreadable contrast;
* inconsistent component sizes;
* poor density;
* excessive whitespace;
* broken responsive layouts;
* and controls that visually appear interactive but are not.

16. Error Handling and Recovery
Normal failure must be designed for. Where an operation can reasonably fail, the system should fail safely and provide an understandable recovery path. Errors should not ordinarily result in:
* silent data loss;
* corrupted state;
* indefinitely blocked interfaces;
* uncontrolled retry loops;
* duplicate destructive operations;
* unexplained crashes;
* or unrecoverable partial state.

17. Data Integrity
Persistent user data must be treated conservatively. The agent must avoid implementation choices that could reasonably cause accidental:
* deletion;
* duplication;
* corruption;
* truncation;
* unintended migration;
* or irreversible transformation

of user data. Schema or format migrations should, where appropriate:
* preserve existing data;
* be deterministic;
* support interrupted execution safely;
* avoid running repeatedly where not intended;
* and provide a recovery or compatibility strategy.

18. Security and Privacy
Security must be considered during implementation rather than deferred until after feature development. Where relevant, the agent must account for:
* authentication;
* authorisation;
* privilege boundaries;
* secret storage;
* input validation;
* path traversal;
* injection;
* cross-site scripting;
* request forgery;
* unsafe deserialisation;
* arbitrary code execution;
* dependency risks;
* sensitive logging;
* and unintended data exposure.

19. External Side Effects
Actions affecting external systems must be treated differently from reversible local edits. Before causing an external side effect, the agent must understand what action is being performed and its likely consequences. Examples include:
* publishing software;
* sending communications;
* modifying production infrastructure;
* deleting remote resources;
* charging money;
* purchasing services;
* changing account permissions;
* altering public content;
* or deploying externally visible changes.

20. Dependencies and Third-Party Software
Dependencies must be chosen deliberately. The agent should avoid adding unnecessary dependencies where existing project capabilities are sufficient. Before introducing a significant dependency, the agent should consider:
* maintenance status;
* licensing;
* platform compatibility;
* security;
* project size;
* architectural fit;
* and whether the functionality already exists elsewhere in the project.

22. Architecture and Maintainability
The agent must implement functionality in a maintainable way consistent with the architecture of the project. It should avoid:
* unnecessary duplication;
* giant monolithic files where abstraction is appropriate;
* deeply coupled components without reason;
* temporary hacks presented as permanent architecture;
* magic constants where configuration is appropriate;
* inconsistent naming;
* parallel implementations of the same capability;
* and unnecessary framework circumvention.
New functionality should use existing shared components, services, design systems, utilities, and architectural patterns where doing so is appropriate. Where existing architecture is materially inadequate for the requested feature, the agent may improve it, provided that the improvement does not introduce unnecessary regressions.

23. Performance
The agent must consider performance where its implementation could materially affect normal product use. Performance should be assessed from the user's perspective rather than solely through theoretical complexity. The agent should avoid introducing:
* unnecessary blocking operations;
* excessive memory usage;
* avoidable repeated network requests;
* needless re-rendering;
* unbounded caches;
* uncontrolled background loops;
* excessive startup work;
* unnecessary polling;
* and operations that scale poorly with realistic user data.
Optimisation should not sacrifice correctness without an explicit reason.

Where the agent delegates work to subagents, workers, or parallel development processes, the supervising agent remains responsible for the resulting work. Delegation does not transfer responsibility for:
* correctness;
* integration;
* testing;
* architecture;
* source-control safety;
* consistency;
* or compliance with this contract.
Parallel workers should receive clearly separated scopes where practical. Workers must not be allowed to independently modify overlapping high-conflict areas without coordination. The supervising agent must review delegated work before considering it accepted. Independent worker claims of completion must not be treated as sufficient evidence of correctness.

Where the user requests autonomous operation, autonomy must exist in practice rather than merely in branding. An autonomous system should, within its authorised scope:
* select appropriate next actions;
* continue after successful intermediate steps;
* recover from routine failures;
* avoid repeatedly asking questions whose answers can reasonably be determined from available context;
* recognise completed work;
* detect blocked work;
* avoid infinite loops;
* and produce usable state for later continuation.
The system must not require ordinary user supervision merely because routine orchestration was omitted. Autonomy does not authorise the system to exceed its permissions, disregard safety requirements, or make irreversible external decisions outside the user's granted scope.

Autonomous agents must detect when they are no longer making meaningful progress. The agent should recognise patterns such as:
* repeatedly applying and reverting the same change;
* repeatedly running the same failing command without new information;
* recreating already completed work;
* continuously rediscovering the same blocker;
* oscillating between incompatible approaches;
* spawning uncontrolled recursive workers;
* or retrying an unavailable dependency indefinitely.
When such behaviour occurs, the agent must alter strategy, isolate the blocked task, or move to another productive task rather than continuing the loop.

Questions should be reserved for genuinely consequential ambiguity. Minor implementation details should be resolved using:
1. explicit project requirements;
2. existing project conventions;
3. donor or upstream behaviour where applicable;
4. established industry conventions;
5. and reasonable engineering judgement,
in that order. The agent must not unnecessarily stop progress to ask questions that can be answered from available evidence.

Completion claims must be supported by evidence. Where appropriate, a completed development pass should be able to identify:
* files or components changed;
* significant functionality added or corrected;
* tests run;
* build status;
* runtime validation performed;
* visual QA performed;
* known remaining limitations;
* and any environmental gates that could not be completed.
The purpose of this requirement is not to generate excessive reports. It is to ensure that completion can be distinguished from assumption. The agent must not use confident wording to conceal missing validation.

The agent must not introduce placeholder implementation into production paths unless explicitly requested. This includes:
* TODO-only functionality;
* fake buttons;
* dead navigation;
* dummy API responses;
* hard-coded example results;
* temporary mock services;
* lorem ipsum;
* placeholder graphics;
* knowingly non-functional settings;
* or interfaces for features that do not exist.
Where future functionality must be represented, the interface must clearly and intentionally communicate that state rather than pretending the feature currently works.

Features must be integrated into the actual product. Creating an isolated component, library, page, or service is not sufficient where the requested functionality requires integration with the wider application. The agent must consider relevant:
* navigation;
* persistence;
* state management;
* permissions;
* application lifecycle;
* search;
* settings;
* interoperability;
* keyboard commands;
* undo/redo behaviour;
* import/export;
* and cross-feature workflows.
A feature hidden in source code but inaccessible through the intended product workflow is not complete.

Where multiple product surfaces share a design system, capability, data model, or interaction concept, new work must remain consistent with the rest of the product unless a deliberate difference is required. The agent should reuse established:
* terminology;
* icons;
* spacing;
* shortcuts;
* menus;
* modal behaviour;
* context menus;
* drag-and-drop conventions;
* selection behaviour;
* settings organisation;
* and common components.
Equivalent actions should behave equivalently across the product.

The agent should validate complete workflows rather than only isolated implementation details. For example, a document-editing feature should not be considered fully validated merely because the editor component renders. A realistic workflow might include:
1. creating or opening a document;
2. editing it;
3. saving it;
4. closing it;
5. reopening it;
6. verifying persistence;
7. performing relevant secondary actions;
8. and confirming errors do not appear during ordinary use.
The exact workflow MUST reflect how a real user is expected to use the feature, and APP-AC governs the evidence and stage claim. Successful create/edit/save/reopen of plain text alone does not demonstrate a word processor, nor does one isolated happy path prove donor parity or complete application functionality.

A task may be declared done only when, within its requested scope:
* implementation is substantive;
* requested functionality exists;
* user-facing behaviour is integrated;
* expected workflows operate;
* important failure cases are handled;
* existing functionality has not been materially regressed;
* relevant tests pass;
* visual quality has been inspected where applicable;
* accessibility has been considered;
* security implications have been considered;
* documentation is updated where needed;
* placeholders do not substitute for requested functionality;
* and completion claims can be supported by evidence.
Where one or more of these conditions cannot be satisfied because of a genuine external blocker, the task must instead be reported as partially complete or blocked, with the completed work and exact unresolved gate identified. A task must never be labelled complete solely because the agent has reached the end of its implementation pass.

Business add-ons — pricing, seats and resource billing
Business costs an additional USD $5 per month per organisation and supports a maximum of 50 seats. Business + costs an additional USD $15 per month per organisation and has no subscription seat cap. Neither add-on has a separate per-seat fee. Purchased AI usage/credits, storage and other funded resources remain separately configured and may be pooled or allocated individually; unlimited seats do not mean unlimited resources.
Both add-ons include 9to1 Admin and the functional Business capabilities specified below. Business + includes the Business capability set; further exclusive Business + capabilities are OPEN and MUST NOT be invented from its name. Pricing displays, quotes, invoices and entitlement APIs MUST distinguish the organisation add-on, seat capacity and resource charges. A base-resource quote in another currency MUST NOT be silently relabelled as USD; the trusted billing quote must make currency/conversion explicit.
Billing state MUST store AddOnID, add-on definition version, OrgID, seat-limit value or explicit Unlimited, effective dates and applicable account-bound grants separately from user resource configurations. No client-supplied role, username or plan label can self-grant Business. The free Business entitlement for Jacob remains governed by the Profiles subsection and does not authorise paid resource purchases or third-party spending.
Changing Business + to Business with more than 50 active members MUST require a seat-remediation preview or retain a visibly constrained pending-downgrade state; it must not silently remove users or delete shared content. Add-on cancellation and billing failure MUST have explicit entitlement/recovery states. Account-level billing, export and recovery remain available through appropriate authorised routes even when normal Business editing is unavailable. Exact grace periods, proration and suspended/pending-seat accounting remain OPEN unless defined in the canonical billing policy.
